## Purpose
- Pin down the auth API contract and token/cookie behavior.

## Source of truth
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Controllers/AuthController*.cs`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Configuration/JwtOptions.cs`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/.env.example`
- `frontend/team-hub-web/src/app/core/auth/auth.service.ts`

## Do
- Use base route `/api/auth/v0.0` (API version `0.0`).
- Keep these endpoints:
  - `POST /api/auth/v0.0/register` -> `201 Created`, `400 Bad Request` on format validation errors (`ValidationProblemDetails`, `type=…/validation-failed`), `409 Conflict` on business validation errors (duplicate username/email) with `errors` payload (`type=…/conflict`).
  - `POST /api/auth/v0.0/login` -> `200 OK` with access token + user; request accepts `rememberMe` (boolean) to control persistent refresh cookie; errors as RFC 9457 `application/problem+json` (`invalid-credentials` / `account-locked` with `code`, `remainingAttempts`, `lockoutSeconds` extensions); `503 Service Unavailable` when Redis session store is down.
  - `POST /api/auth/v0.0/refresh` -> `200 OK` with rotated refresh token cookie, `401 Unauthorized` ProblemDetails if invalid/missing; `503 Service Unavailable` when Redis session store is down.
  - `POST /api/auth/v0.0/logout` -> `204 No Content` and cookie cleanup.
  - `POST /api/auth/v0.0/change-password` -> `204 No Content` after username + name + surname verification; `400 Bad Request` on format validation errors; `401 Unauthorized` ProblemDetails when identity verification fails.
  - `GET /api/auth/v0.0/users` -> `200 OK` with all user profiles (`id`, `username`, `name`, `surname`) ordered by username; requires JWT Bearer; `401 Unauthorized` when missing/invalid.
- Keep register validation:
  - format (`400`): `name`, `surname`, `username`, `password` field rules.
  - business (`409`): duplicate `username` in `errors.username` (case-insensitive).
- Keep refresh token in `refreshToken` HttpOnly cookie (`SameSite=Lax`); when `rememberMe=false`, keep it session-scoped.
- Store refresh sessions in Redis (`auth:session:{refreshTokenHash}`) with TTL matching refresh token expiry.
- Keep `withCredentials: true` for login/refresh/logout in frontend clients.
- Keep JWT options from env/config: `Jwt__Key`, `Jwt__Issuer`, `Jwt__Audience`, `Jwt:ExpireMinutes`.
- Keep Redis connection from infrastructure: `Redis__ConnectionString` (see `infrastructure/redis/.env.example`; docker compose sets `redis:6379` for auth).
- Keep PostgreSQL connection: `ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection` in auth `.env` (docker compose prod: `Host=postgres;...;Database=authdb;...`; local `dotnet run`: `Host=localhost;Port=5433;Database=auth_db;...`).
- Keep auth health endpoint: `GET /health` checks PostgreSQL and Redis (`200` healthy, `503` unhealthy).

## Don't
- Do not return refresh token in response body.
- Do not loosen cookie security flags without explicit requirement.
- Do not introduce undocumented auth endpoints.

## Checklist
- Endpoint names and status codes match controller code/tests.
- Cookie name remains exactly `refreshToken`.
- JWT options are present before app start (`ValidateOnStart`).
